WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
GRETCHEN: Peter J. Leithart
is president of the Theopolis

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.840
Institute in Birmingham, Alabama
where he also serves as teacher

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.240
at Trinity Presbyterian Church.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.900
He received a BA in English and
history from Hillsdale College,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.350
a master of arts in religion,
and a master of theology

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:19.620
from Westminster Theological
Seminary, and a PhD

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.990
from the University
of Cambridge.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.870
He has served as pastor of
Reformed Heritage Presbyterian

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:27.810
Church, now Trinity
Presbyterian Church,

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.640
and is Pastor of
Trinity reformed

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.710
Church in Moscow, Idaho.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:35.070
A former professor of theology
and literature at New Saint

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.950
Andrew's College and a biweekly
columnist for First Things, Dr.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.470
Leithart is the author
of numerous books,

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:44.550
including a two-volume
commentary on Revelation,

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.500
and most recently, Baptism--

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.050
A Guide to Life from Death.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.519
Please join me in
welcoming Dr. Leithart.

00:00:51.519 --> 00:00:53.964
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.570
PETER LEITHART:
Thank you, Gretchen.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.489
Thanks to Matt Bell,
and thanks to everyone

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:09.970
associated with CCA
for inviting me.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.290
As Gretchen mentioned, I am a
graduate of Hillsdale College.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:18.630
I realized, to my shock
earlier this week,

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:23.100
that it's been 40 years
since I graduated.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.568
That's a shocking number.

00:01:26.568 --> 00:01:27.860
It's very good to be back here.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.000
It's a very different
school than I attended,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.370
as you can imagine.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:33.630
None of this existed.

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.030
Many of the buildings
didn't exist.

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.040
And it's always a
delight to be back here,

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.220
and to get reacquainted
with some old friends,

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.830
and to be able to speak to you--

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:47.550
students and other
visitors, to the CCA.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.800
I want to do a couple of
preliminaries before I begin.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.220
The first is my usual
disclaimer whenever

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.350
I talk about Jane Austen.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.690
And the disclaimer
is that I know

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:59.550
there are dozens
in this room who

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:03.150
know Jane Austen's
novels better than I do,

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:06.420
so I beg your indulgence.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.039
The second
preliminary, I'm going

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:11.070
to try to get you to
do something for me.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:14.740
I wrote a book called Miniatures
and Morals some years ago,

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.280
which is a study of Jane
Austen's six published novels.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.470
And I titled my introduction
"Real Men Read Austen."

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.840
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:28.530
Because, of course, men,
especially young men,

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:31.710
think that they're girly
books, but they're not.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.830
And so I've tried this
before with not much success.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.560
I've tried to get a
crowd to chant with me,

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:39.030
"Real men read Austen."

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:39.900
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.590
So I'm going to say it once,
and then I want you to join in.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:47.190
And three times I want you to
say, "Real men read Austen."

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:50.490
I'll set the rhythm by
saying it myself first.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.890
Real men read Austen.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:55.620
ALL: Real men read Austen.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.320
Real men read Austen.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.197
Real men read Austen.

00:03:01.197 --> 00:03:02.280
PETER LEITHART: Thank you.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.270
That was far more successful
than the last attempt.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.490
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:12.030
And I hope you young men
and older men out there

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:17.010
who have not read Austen
are now ashamed of your lack

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.000
of reading.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.910
My topic tonight is
morality and Jane Austen.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.320
And I am going to read my paper.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.110
I'll try to make some
eye contact occasionally.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.960
But just to make sure I
stay within my time limit

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.180
and say everything that
I had planned to say,

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.625
I'll be reading.

00:03:31.625 --> 00:03:33.000
Let me outline
what I have to say

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.280
about Jane Austen and morality.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.960
First, I want to examine the
miniaturism of Austen's style

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.910
and storytelling.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:44.640
Second, I'll explain how
her miniaturism determines

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.950
the scope of her moral concerns.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.470
By that, I mean the
zone within which

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.310
the moral life is lived out.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:55.640
Third, I want to show that
Austen's literary style

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:59.060
expresses the substance
of her moral outlook.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:03.230
Style isn't merely a surface
phenomenon for Austen.

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:04.970
Rather, Austen's
style exemplifies

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:08.690
a view of the world in a
moral perspective and models

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:12.140
the comic discernment and
truth telling that she wishes

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:15.420
to instill in her readers.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.579
A well-known quotation
from another woman writer

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:20.279
will orient us at the outset.

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:24.000
In an 1850 letter to
the editor, WS Williams,

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.330
Charlotte Bronte complains that
Austen's novels are altogether

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.040
too tranquil.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.370
Austen "ruffles her readers
by nothing vehement,

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.890
disturbs them with
nothing profound.

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.950
The passions are
perfectly unknown to her.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.320
She rejects even a
speaking acquaintance

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.410
with that stormy sisterhood."

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.090
Austen's problem,
in Bronte's view,

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.330
is that she keeps to
surfaces and never penetrates

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:49.860
emotional depths.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.540
This is Bronte again.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:54.540
"Her business is not half
so much with the human heart

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:58.110
as with human eyes,
mouth, hands, and feet.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:02.880
What sees keenly, speaks
aptly, moves flexibly,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.960
it suits her to study.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:08.100
But what throbs fast
and full, though hidden,

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.360
what the blood rushes through,
what is the unseen seat of life

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.490
and the sentient
target of death,

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:17.970
that, Miss Austen ignores."

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:21.090
Bronte musters only this
backhanded compliment.

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.190
"She does her business
of delineating

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:26.670
the surface of the lives
of genteel English people

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.680
curiously well.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.180
There is a Chinese fidelity,
a miniature delicacy

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.170
in the painting."

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:37.930
Now, I think there's much
wrong with Bronte's assessment

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:38.890
of Austen.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:41.020
But she also gets
something right.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:42.870
And though Bronte would
not have known it,

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:46.630
Austen describes her own work
in remarkably similar terms

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:49.420
in a letter that she wrote
to her nephew, James Edward

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:51.470
Austen-Leigh.

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:54.860
James, her nephew, had
misplaced 2 and 1/2 chapters

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.040
of a manuscript he
had been working on,

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.720
and he had mentioned that
in a letter to Aunt Jane.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:03.620
And Aunt Jane sent this
teasing letter back.

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:06.230
"It is well that I have not
been at Steventon lately,"

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:09.170
she writes, "and therefore,
cannot be suspected

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:11.460
of purloining them.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:14.330
Two strong twigs and a half
towards a nest of my own

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:16.130
would've been something.

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:18.380
I do not think, however,
that any theft of that sort

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:20.600
would have really been
very useful to me.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:23.330
What should I do with your
strong, manly, vigorous

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:26.150
sketches, full of
variety and glow?

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:29.600
How could I possibly join them
onto the little bit of ivory

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.050
on which I work
with so fine a brush

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:37.720
as produces little
effect after much labor?"

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.240
We know from other letters
that Austen made a deliberate

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.270
decision to devote
her life to brushwork

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:46.810
on two-inch bits of ivory.

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:49.430
In a letter to her niece,
Anna, another writer,

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:51.790
Austen says, "You are now
collecting your people

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:54.910
delightfully, getting them
exactly into such a spot

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:56.770
as is the delight of my life.

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:58.990
Three or four families
in a country village

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:01.660
is the very thing to work on."

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:03.160
In another letter,
she advises Anna

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:05.200
to write about what she knows.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.540
She advises her not to take
her characters that she's

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.340
writing about to Ireland.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:11.292
"Let the Portmans go to Ireland.

00:07:11.292 --> 00:07:13.000
But as you know nothing
of manners there,

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.160
you had better not go with them.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:18.960
You will be in danger of
giving false representations."

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:22.200
In reply to Reverend James
Stanier Clarke's suggestion

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:25.350
that she write a
romance, Austen bluntly

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:27.300
insists that she'll
continue to paint

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.420
"such pictures of domestic
life in country villages

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.180
as I deal in."

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.920
And she continues, "I could
no more write a romance

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.420
than an epic poem.

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:40.350
I could not sit down seriously
to write a serious romance

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:43.560
under any other motive
than to save my life.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:45.300
And if it were
indispensable for me

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:48.510
to keep it up and never
relax into laughing at myself

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:51.660
or other people, I am sure
I should be hung before I

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:53.850
had finished the first chapter.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:57.150
No, I must keep to my
own style and my own way.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:59.970
And though I may never
succeed again in that,

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:04.540
I am convinced that I should
totally fail in any other."

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:08.140
Austen's deliberate
miniaturism, her focus on three

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:10.180
or four families in
a country village,

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:14.890
is evident in virtually every
facet of her literary art.

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:17.950
She limits her novels with
respect to action, so much so

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:22.000
that it's often said, especially
by male readers I suppose,

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.600
that nothing happens
in Austen's stories.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:28.030
That's true, if you're expecting
overturned carriages, car

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:30.340
chases, or perhaps zombies.

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:32.320
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.510
There's remarkably
little violence or even

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:36.090
vigorous action.

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:37.539
At worst, Marianne
Dashwood falls

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:41.620
and sprains her ankle on a hill
and later catches a bad cold.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:43.179
Or Louisa Musgrove
falls off a wall

00:08:43.179 --> 00:08:45.490
and gets a nasty
bump on her head.

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:47.290
One critic has said
the most violent thing

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:50.170
that happens in Pride and
Prejudice occurs when Elizabeth

00:08:50.170 --> 00:08:53.880
jumps over a stile on the
way to visit her sister Jane

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:56.002
at Netherfield Park.

00:08:56.002 --> 00:08:57.710
In Sense and Sensibility,
Colonel Brandon

00:08:57.710 --> 00:08:59.660
duels with Willoughby.

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:01.340
Note neither one is injured.

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:03.560
But that scene is not shown.

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.030
And it's spoken
of so cryptically

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:08.750
and briefly that
readers easily miss it.

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:11.300
No one, as far as I recall
or been able to find,

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:14.630
ever bleeds in an Austen novel.

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:18.380
But most importantly, Austen
is a miniaturist of style.

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:21.680
She does more with less than
any other English writer.

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:25.340
There's a precision and lack
of ornamentation in her prose

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.650
that I suspect owes
much to the Bible

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:30.190
and the Book of Common Prayer.

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:32.990
This makes her simply the
most elegant prose stylist

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:36.950
and one of the most innovative
in English literature.

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:38.990
After reading Austen,
every other writer

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:43.460
seems bloated, even, if
I dare to blaspheme as

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:46.470
stylish a writer as CS Lewis.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:48.070
Let me give-- yes,
oh, I heard that.

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:49.080
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:53.520
I heard the rejoinders forming.

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:56.535
Let me give several
examples to make my point.

00:09:56.535 --> 00:09:58.410
First, Austen pays her
readers the compliment

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:01.560
of not spilling out every
detail of the plots.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:04.140
She would've made a great
mystery novelist, I think,

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:06.330
because she expects her
readers to draw conclusions

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:09.117
from the limited information
that she provides.

00:10:09.117 --> 00:10:10.700
Toward the end of
Pride and Prejudice,

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:12.242
for example, Lady
Catherine de Bourgh

00:10:12.242 --> 00:10:14.550
shows up at Longbourn,
as we saw in the movie

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:17.100
yesterday, to pressure
Elizabeth Darcy to give up

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.410
all hope of marrying Mr. Darcy.

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:22.740
During this spirited exchange--
which does not in the original

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:25.260
become a ninja battle--

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.150
during this spirited
exchange, Lady Catherine

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:30.000
reveals that she had received
a report about the engagement

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.895
two days ago.

00:10:31.895 --> 00:10:33.270
Earlier, she had
told Mrs. Bennet

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:37.153
that she left the Collinses
the night before last.

00:10:37.153 --> 00:10:38.820
Elizabeth later puts
the pieces together

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:41.850
and realizes that
Lady Catherine heard

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:44.310
the rumor of Darcy's
impending proposal

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:46.562
from Mr. And Mrs. Collins.

00:10:46.562 --> 00:10:48.270
But Lady Catherine
gives us all the facts

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:52.020
we need to put two and
two together ourselves.

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:54.330
Similarly, at several
points in Emma,

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:57.510
Miss Bates provides
crucial information

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.330
about the puzzling relationship
between Frank Churchill

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:02.220
and Jane Fairfax.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:05.010
But we need to pay careful
attention to her rambling

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:07.440
gossip to get the point.

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:09.390
Few readers do.

00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:14.580
We, as readers, are as impatient
with Miss Bates as Emma is.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:16.410
Also, at the end of
Emma, unlike the movie

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:19.500
we saw this
afternoon, Mrs. Elton

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:22.200
does complain about the lack
of elegance at Emma's wedding,

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:24.150
but the narrator tells
us that she was not

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.160
even at the wedding.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:28.140
It says that she drew
her conclusions, quote,

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:32.970
"from the particulars
detailed by her husband."

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:35.040
Austen frequently
leaves settings

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:37.500
to the reader's
imagination as well.

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:40.200
Writing to her niece, Anna,
she offers this advice.

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:43.170
"You describe a sweet place,
but your descriptions are often

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:45.270
more minute than will be liked.

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:48.680
You give too many particulars
of right hand and left."

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:50.090
Austen often
creates the illusion

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:52.430
of a scene with a few
strokes and props,

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:56.730
like a caricaturist who captures
a face with a line or two.

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:58.740
On Knightley's first
visit to Hartfield

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:00.960
at the beginning of
Emma, nothing in the room

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:03.870
is mentioned besides a
backgammon table, a fire,

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:04.975
and the visitor.

00:12:04.975 --> 00:12:07.350
There's no description of
Knightley's physical appearance

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:08.490
or of the room.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:10.180
And even some of the
props in the room,

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:12.990
some of the objects in the room,
are mentioned by the characters

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:15.660
rather than by the narrator.

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:18.480
Austen rounds off
characters with an economy

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:20.693
that borders on the miraculous.

00:12:20.693 --> 00:12:22.110
After a little
more than two pages

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:24.390
of dialogue at the beginning
Pride and Prejudice,

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:27.870
we know Mr. And Mrs.
Bennet, their relationship

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:29.370
with each other,
their relationships

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.590
with their daughters,
their hopes, especially

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:34.350
Mrs. Bennet's hopes,
their faults and follies,

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:39.490
Mrs. Bennet's nerves, and Mr.
Bennet's sardonic distance.

00:12:39.490 --> 00:12:42.250
This compressed
characterization frequently

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:47.150
expresses Austen's conviction
that syntax is character.

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:51.790
How someone speaks manifests
his moral quality as much as

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:54.010
or even more than what he says.

00:12:57.100 --> 00:12:59.530
Before Mr. Collins appears
in Pride and Prejudice,

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:01.420
he's been introduced
by a letter that he

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:05.110
wrote in which he discusses his
breach with the Bennet family

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:08.340
and his eventual inheritance of
the Bennet home at Longbourn.

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:10.470
This is from his letter.

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:13.560
"The disagreement
subsisting between yourself

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:18.140
and my late honored father
always gave me much uneasiness.

00:13:18.140 --> 00:13:20.460
And since I have had the
misfortune to lose him,

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:22.830
I have frequently wished
to heal the breach.

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:25.560
But for some time, I was
kept back by my own doubts,

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:28.717
fearing lest it might seem
disrespectful to his memory

00:13:28.717 --> 00:13:30.300
for me to be on good
terms with anyone

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:33.390
with whom it always pleased
him to be at variance.

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:35.430
As a clergyman, moreover,
I feel it my duty

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:37.890
to promote and establish
the blessing of peace

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:40.820
in all families within
the reach of my influence.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:42.420
And on these grounds,
I flatter myself

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:44.490
that my present
overtures of good will

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:48.060
are highly commendable, that the
circumstance of my being next

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:50.910
in the entail at Longbourn
will be kindly overlooked

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:55.700
on your side and not lead you
to reject the olive branch."

00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:59.960
Anyone whose speech or writing
is as convoluted and orotund

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:03.080
as Collins' cannot be sensible.

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:04.730
Elizabeth gets
him exactly right.

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:06.860
Just by hearing the
letter, she knows "There's

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:09.410
something pompous in his style.

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:13.280
And if the style is
pompous, so is the man."

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:16.430
For Austen's characters,
just as for Austen herself,

00:14:16.430 --> 00:14:19.850
style is substantive.

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:23.150
One of my favorite examples
of this principle of syntax

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:25.820
and character, the connection
between syntax and character,

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:29.390
comes from Emma, where Austen
relates Mrs. Elton's stream

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:31.970
of consciousness during the
strawberry picking outing

00:14:31.970 --> 00:14:34.310
at Knightley's Donwell Hall.

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:36.790
This is Mrs. Elton.

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:38.500
"The best fruit in England--

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:41.300
everybody's favorite--
always wholesome.

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:44.080
These, the finest beds
and the finest sorts--

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:46.180
delightful to
gather for oneself--

00:14:46.180 --> 00:14:48.370
the only way of
really enjoying them.

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:50.410
Morning decidedly
the best time--

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:53.490
never tired-- every sort good--

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:56.410
hautboy infinitely
superior-- no comparison--

00:14:56.410 --> 00:14:58.840
the others hardly eatable--

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:00.940
hautboy very scarce--

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:04.210
Chili preferred-- white
wood finest flavor of all--

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:06.310
price of strawberries
in London--

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:07.990
abundance about Bristol--

00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:10.270
Maple Grove-- cultivation--

00:15:10.270 --> 00:15:11.950
beds when to be renewed--

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:15.160
gardeners thinking exactly
different-- no general rule--

00:15:15.160 --> 00:15:17.380
gardeners never to be
put out of their way--

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:21.550
delicious fruit-- only
too rich to eat much of--

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:25.750
inferior to cherries--
currants more refreshing--

00:15:25.750 --> 00:15:28.930
only objection to gathering
strawberries the stooping--

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:32.290
glaring sun-- tired to death--

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:34.030
could bear it no longer--

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:37.380
must go and sit in the shade."

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:40.230
Mrs. Elton is all there--

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:43.530
the contradictory opinions,
each expressed with utter

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:48.750
confidence, the randomness,
the reversion to her obsessive

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:51.690
themes, like her
home of Maple Grove,

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:54.630
the domineering
know-it-all-ness.

00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:58.350
One fragmented paragraph
captures a whole person.

00:15:58.350 --> 00:16:01.410
And again, it's not
what she's saying,

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:06.390
but how her mind moves
that reveals her character.

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:08.730
One indication of
Collins' poverty of style,

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:12.450
and hence of his mind,
is poverty of metaphor.

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:16.110
Austen has been characterized as
a relentlessly non-metaphorical

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:18.600
writer, but I think
that's misleading.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:21.000
Though she rarely uses
explicit similes or metaphors,

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:25.140
her language is metaphorical
in a more profound sense.

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:28.230
She has an intuitive grasp
of the inherent relatedness

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:29.460
of things.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:32.490
And she often adapts language
from one realm of life

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:35.630
to describe another, which
is the essence of metaphor.

00:16:35.630 --> 00:16:38.160
When she wishes, she's
capable of producing

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:40.980
striking metaphorical formulas.

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:43.440
In Emma, during the
strawberry picking scene,

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:46.500
she writes of Mrs.
Elton's bonnet and basket

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:50.720
as her "apparatus of happiness."

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:52.760
She describes the aftermath
of Elton's proposal

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:55.910
to Emma by saying that "their
straightforward emotions left

00:16:55.910 --> 00:17:00.300
no room for the little
zigzags of embarrassment."

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:02.010
And in Sense and
Sensibility, she

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:06.270
refers to the "puppyism"
of Robert Ferrar's manners.

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:09.450
None of these is
formally a metaphor,

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:12.450
but they all manifest
Austen's profound metaphorical

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:14.040
imagination.

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:16.770
What Austen despises
is not metaphor,

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:19.349
but metaphors that are
so overworked that they

00:17:19.349 --> 00:17:21.420
ought have been retired.

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:23.310
In a letter to her
niece, Anna, she

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:26.900
warns against using the phrase
"vortex of dissipation."

00:17:26.900 --> 00:17:30.500
And she explains, "I do
not object to the thing,

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:32.820
but I cannot bear
the expression.

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:37.740
It is such thorough
novel slang and so old

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:41.782
that I daresay Adam met with it
in the first novel he opened."

00:17:41.782 --> 00:17:43.680
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:45.930
Collins' use of
"olive branch" is

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:49.440
a sign of his folly, for that
metaphor is as old as Noah,

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:51.270
if not Adam.

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:52.925
Metaphors tend to
die and fossilize.

00:17:52.925 --> 00:17:57.360
And once that happens, they
can be used without thought.

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:00.240
Dead metaphors shift
the mind into autopilot

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:04.920
and thus hinder the arduous
labor of discernment.

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:06.310
In a sense, Bronte was right.

00:18:06.310 --> 00:18:09.790
Austen delineates the
surfaces of English life,

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:11.860
and she does it
without embellishment

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:15.250
in restrained,
crystalline prose.

00:18:15.250 --> 00:18:18.460
But this doesn't make
her a superficial writer,

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:20.050
because her
attention to surfaces

00:18:20.050 --> 00:18:23.560
opens up an attention
to substantive truth

00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:25.660
and moral insight.

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:27.310
Behind Austen's
aesthetic decision

00:18:27.310 --> 00:18:29.410
to limit herself
to what she knows

00:18:29.410 --> 00:18:33.040
is what could be called a
philosophical stance that's

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:35.510
vaguely nominalist.

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:38.300
Particulars, Austen
sensed, are all

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:41.870
we can talk about with any
degree of accuracy or detail.

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:43.640
About universals, we
can say very little,

00:18:43.640 --> 00:18:48.610
since universals, by definition,
lack particular qualities.

00:18:48.610 --> 00:18:50.660
In Northanger Abbey,
Henry Tilney's discourse

00:18:50.660 --> 00:18:55.660
on the theory of the picturesque
ends ultimately in silence.

00:18:55.660 --> 00:18:57.610
"Delighted with Catherine
Morland's progress,"

00:18:57.610 --> 00:19:00.580
Austen writes, "and
fearful of wearying her

00:19:00.580 --> 00:19:04.060
with too much wisdom at once,
Henry suffered the subject

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:05.290
to decline.

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:08.590
And by an easy transition
from a piece of rocky fragment

00:19:08.590 --> 00:19:10.990
and the withered oak which he
had placed near its summit,

00:19:10.990 --> 00:19:14.170
to oaks in general, to
forests, the inclosure of them,

00:19:14.170 --> 00:19:16.950
wastelands, crown
lands, and governments,

00:19:16.950 --> 00:19:19.660
he shortly found himself
arrived at politics.

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:24.910
And from politics, it was
an easy step to silence."

00:19:24.910 --> 00:19:27.490
The broader the scope
of discourse for Austen,

00:19:27.490 --> 00:19:30.910
the less opportunity there is
for the fine discrimination,

00:19:30.910 --> 00:19:32.890
for the nuances and
shades of difference that

00:19:32.890 --> 00:19:37.113
are necessary for true
knowledge and sound judgment.

00:19:37.113 --> 00:19:38.530
The larger the
scope of discourse,

00:19:38.530 --> 00:19:43.270
the more everything blurs into
undifferentiated smoothness

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:47.310
that Austen and many of
her characters abhor.

00:19:47.310 --> 00:19:50.100
But it's precisely
this nominalism,

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:53.070
this minute attention to
the surfaces of character

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:55.890
and relation, that makes
Austen's work a continuing

00:19:55.890 --> 00:19:58.050
source of moral instruction.

00:19:58.050 --> 00:20:01.180
Because of her
limitations, she emphasizes

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:05.880
the domestic and local contexts
for moral decisions and action,

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:09.390
the very contexts in which
most of our moral decisions

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:12.080
and actions occur.

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:14.570
For Austen, the sensational
doesn't provide a sound basis

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:16.250
for moral reasoning.

00:20:16.250 --> 00:20:20.150
Hers isn't a lifeboat ethics
focusing on marginal extremes

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:21.650
of decision-making.

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:23.750
On the contrary, she
recognizes that the greatest

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:26.960
moral challenges come in
the midst of daily life,

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:31.000
precisely when
nothing is happening.

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:33.700
Austen's concentration
on the quotidian

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:36.280
is related to what CS
Lewis described as Austen's

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:39.220
untragic moral outlook.

00:20:39.220 --> 00:20:43.030
"Her core convictions are
exacting, insofar as obedience

00:20:43.030 --> 00:20:45.070
is rigidly demanded.

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:48.220
Neither excuses nor
experiments are allowed.

00:20:48.220 --> 00:20:51.820
Austen's principles provide
the grammar of conduct,

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:54.370
being something that
anyone can learn

00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:57.350
and something that
everyone must learn.

00:20:57.350 --> 00:21:00.110
On the other hand, her moral
vision is," Lewis says,

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:02.600
"unexacting, insofar
as the duties

00:21:02.600 --> 00:21:06.140
commanded are not quixotic or
heroic, and obedience to them

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:08.900
will not be very difficult
to properly brought up people

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:11.270
in ordinary circumstances."

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:14.870
Austen is untragic also
in the cheerful moderation

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:16.850
of her joys.

00:21:16.850 --> 00:21:18.620
Again, this is Lewis.

00:21:18.620 --> 00:21:21.380
"If she envisaged a
few great sacrifices,

00:21:21.380 --> 00:21:25.430
she also envisages no
grandiose schemes of joy.

00:21:25.430 --> 00:21:27.710
She has, or at least all
her favorite characters

00:21:27.710 --> 00:21:31.220
have, a hearty relish for
what would now be regarded

00:21:31.220 --> 00:21:33.680
as very modest pleasures--

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:36.920
a ball, a dinner party,
books, conversation,

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:40.520
a drive to see a great house
10 miles away, a holiday as far

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:41.770
away as Derbyshire.

00:21:41.770 --> 00:21:45.832
These, with affection and
good manners, are happiness."

00:21:48.550 --> 00:21:51.910
Unlike post-romantic
novelists and philosophers,

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:54.358
Austen doesn't pose
ultimate questions.

00:21:54.358 --> 00:21:55.900
She doesn't struggle
against the void

00:21:55.900 --> 00:21:58.223
or probe the mysteries
of the universe.

00:21:58.223 --> 00:22:00.640
She doesn't pose these questions
because, I think for her,

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:02.320
they were not questions.

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:03.738
They're settled.

00:22:03.738 --> 00:22:05.530
A lifelong member of
the Church of England,

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:08.260
a preacher's kid,
a priest's kid,

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:09.850
she believed that
the grand questions

00:22:09.850 --> 00:22:13.555
could be answered by consulting
the Thirty-nine Articles.

00:22:13.555 --> 00:22:15.180
For Austen, the moral
challenge of life

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:17.222
is not to puzzle out the
meaning of the universe,

00:22:17.222 --> 00:22:21.240
but to live well in
particular and given

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:24.170
social and domestic settings.

00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:26.420
The moral philosopher,
Alasdair MacIntyre,

00:22:26.420 --> 00:22:30.380
discerns an Aristotelian
strain in Austen's recognition

00:22:30.380 --> 00:22:33.770
that virtues are formed,
tested, and manifested

00:22:33.770 --> 00:22:36.080
within communities.

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:38.090
As Aristotle recognizes,
this makes ethics

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:40.040
a subdivision of politics.

00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:42.350
The question "What should I do?"

00:22:42.350 --> 00:22:45.860
is a sub-question under "What
kind of community do I live in,

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:48.270
and what is my place in it?"

00:22:48.270 --> 00:22:50.370
For both Austen and
Aristotle, the moral life

00:22:50.370 --> 00:22:53.140
is status-specific.

00:22:53.140 --> 00:22:55.850
To answer the question, what
should I do in this case?,

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:58.360
we have to ask, who am I?

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:02.170
And the latter question is not
about some ghostly inner I,

00:23:02.170 --> 00:23:04.540
but about the role
and status that I have

00:23:04.540 --> 00:23:07.140
in a particular social setting.

00:23:07.140 --> 00:23:11.430
Darcy must not only ask, as
a Bronte character might,

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:15.810
"Shall I, who passionately love
Elizabeth Bennet's fine eyes,

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:18.000
pursue Elizabeth Bennet?"

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:21.690
He must also consider the
exterior of the situation.

00:23:21.690 --> 00:23:25.590
"Shall I, with my name and
status as an English nobleman,

00:23:25.590 --> 00:23:29.100
pursue Elizabeth Bennet
with her status, her name,

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:30.917
and her family?"

00:23:30.917 --> 00:23:33.250
When Knightley castigates
Emma for her treatment of Miss

00:23:33.250 --> 00:23:35.350
Bates, as we saw in the
movie this afternoon,

00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:38.050
he challenges her on
precisely this point.

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:40.960
"Consider your position in
the society of the town,"

00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:43.810
he chides, "and the
obligation your position

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:47.560
places on you to show kindness
to an unfortunate, if silly,

00:23:47.560 --> 00:23:49.970
spinster like Miss Bates."

00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:51.800
Morality is not
about individuals

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:54.770
seeking to live a good life,
about solitary decision-makers

00:23:54.770 --> 00:23:57.380
striving for ethical perfection.

00:23:57.380 --> 00:24:00.170
A moral person must
learn to navigate well

00:24:00.170 --> 00:24:03.820
among the surfaces
of social life.

00:24:03.820 --> 00:24:06.340
Given the well-defined strata
of the communities that Austen

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:08.470
deals in, this was a
more obvious question

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:11.180
for her and for her characters
than it might be for us,

00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:15.890
but it's still a central and
perennial moral question.

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:18.200
Deciding what is right is
never simply a matter of,

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:21.690
what should I as
a human being do?

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:23.900
It's always, what should
I as a college student,

00:24:23.900 --> 00:24:28.070
or as a husband or wife, or
as a car mechanic do in this

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:31.370
or that specific situation?

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:32.660
This is not relativism.

00:24:32.660 --> 00:24:35.303
It doesn't mean there are no
absolutes of right and wrong,

00:24:35.303 --> 00:24:36.970
but it does mean that
the absolutes have

00:24:36.970 --> 00:24:39.340
particular application
to particular people

00:24:39.340 --> 00:24:41.740
in particular circumstances.

00:24:41.740 --> 00:24:45.070
Only a sophomoric morality
will ignore the specificity

00:24:45.070 --> 00:24:46.690
of our moral
decisions and actions,

00:24:46.690 --> 00:24:49.733
and Austen was no sophomore.

00:24:49.733 --> 00:24:51.150
In one important
respect, I think,

00:24:51.150 --> 00:24:55.130
Austen's moral vision
surpasses Aristotle's.

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:57.588
In the Nicomachean
Ethics, Aristotle

00:24:57.588 --> 00:24:59.130
distinguishes between
different sorts

00:24:59.130 --> 00:25:04.020
of action, which he describes
as poiesis and praxis.

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:09.080
Production, or poiesis, is
different from praxis, action.

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:13.730
And Aristotle says, "The reason
state that is capable of action

00:25:13.730 --> 00:25:17.320
is also different from that
which is capable of production.

00:25:17.320 --> 00:25:19.060
Hence, neither is
included in the other,

00:25:19.060 --> 00:25:22.390
because action, praxis, is
not poiesis, production,

00:25:22.390 --> 00:25:24.830
nor production action."

00:25:24.830 --> 00:25:28.430
For Aristotle, ethics
deals with praxis,

00:25:28.430 --> 00:25:31.430
with intransitive
action, actions

00:25:31.430 --> 00:25:34.610
that don't produce anything
outside of the actor,

00:25:34.610 --> 00:25:37.100
but bear fruit only in
the character and virtue

00:25:37.100 --> 00:25:39.680
of the actor himself.

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:42.110
Poiesis is the realm
of transitive action,

00:25:42.110 --> 00:25:44.000
like building a table.

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:47.720
That's an action that produces
something outside yourself.

00:25:47.720 --> 00:25:50.810
By Aristotle's distinction,
the realm of the arts

00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:53.630
and of economic production
is, strictly speaking,

00:25:53.630 --> 00:25:56.370
outside the boundaries
of the ethical.

00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:59.220
But I think that
distinction is a mistake--

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:02.010
the assumption that our actions
can be confined to ourselves,

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:05.340
that we can engage in praxis
without producing anything

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:07.440
outside ourselves.

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:09.990
However Aristotelian Austen
is in other respects,

00:26:09.990 --> 00:26:11.730
she implicitly rejects
this distinction,

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:15.450
for she knows implicitly
that every action is poetic,

00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:16.860
it's productive.

00:26:16.860 --> 00:26:18.360
The effects of our
actions are never

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:20.520
confined simply to ourselves.

00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:22.920
We always produce and
proliferate effects

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:24.390
in the world.

00:26:24.390 --> 00:26:27.770
All actions are transitive.

00:26:27.770 --> 00:26:30.920
Austen's villains
don't recognize this.

00:26:30.920 --> 00:26:33.440
Henry and Mary Crawford,
in Mansfield Park,

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:35.870
are individualists in
the sense that they

00:26:35.870 --> 00:26:38.390
follow their own desires,
regardless of what

00:26:38.390 --> 00:26:40.427
the authorities say or do.

00:26:40.427 --> 00:26:42.260
When Sir Thomas Bertram
is away on business,

00:26:42.260 --> 00:26:45.140
they take part in a theatrical
production at Mansfield Park,

00:26:45.140 --> 00:26:47.960
even though they're warned
that the master of the house

00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:50.870
would disapprove, and even
over the initial objections

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:53.150
of Edmund, who's
responsible for maintaining

00:26:53.150 --> 00:26:56.180
the house in his
father's absence.

00:26:56.180 --> 00:26:58.820
More subtly, the
Crawfords have no sense

00:26:58.820 --> 00:27:03.680
that their actions have
consequences beyond themselves.

00:27:03.680 --> 00:27:08.180
When Henry runs off with Maria
Bertram, now Mrs. Rushworth,

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:11.270
Mary Crawford still hopes
that Edmund will marry her,

00:27:11.270 --> 00:27:12.490
will marry Mary.

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:14.810
She's utterly
insensible to the fact

00:27:14.810 --> 00:27:18.920
that her brother's scandal
might affect her in some way.

00:27:21.500 --> 00:27:25.540
She sees herself as completely
disconnected from her brother's

00:27:25.540 --> 00:27:28.040
immoral action.

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:30.830
Austen's characters experience
moral illumination and growth,

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:33.020
as we heard last night
from James Bowman.

00:27:33.020 --> 00:27:36.040
And her novels are designed
to train readers as well.

00:27:36.040 --> 00:27:39.050
And I want to close with a few
observations about how this

00:27:39.050 --> 00:27:41.780
is so, how her novels
are designed to be

00:27:41.780 --> 00:27:44.150
moral training for the reader.

00:27:44.150 --> 00:27:45.650
First, since the
moral life is lived

00:27:45.650 --> 00:27:50.390
within a particular
social setting,

00:27:50.390 --> 00:27:54.950
Austen is deeply concerned with
moral education and guidance.

00:27:54.950 --> 00:27:58.610
Moral guides are needed, in part
because language and manners

00:27:58.610 --> 00:28:01.560
can function as disguises.

00:28:01.560 --> 00:28:04.020
The critic, Tony Tanner,
has made the point well

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:05.820
in his book on Austen.

00:28:05.820 --> 00:28:08.370
For Austen, good manners
are essential to preserving

00:28:08.370 --> 00:28:10.980
social order and
living the moral life.

00:28:10.980 --> 00:28:12.840
Yet, in the very
places and times

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:16.000
when manners are
most on display,

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:19.280
they are most difficult
to read correctly.

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:21.820
A ball is an occasion
for a country village

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:26.380
to parade, enact, and
practice its codes of conduct.

00:28:26.380 --> 00:28:29.710
A ball is an inherently
theatrical occasion

00:28:29.710 --> 00:28:32.170
when everyone is
acting according

00:28:32.170 --> 00:28:34.780
to the script of manners.

00:28:34.780 --> 00:28:38.830
But then how can you tell when
somebody is acting his acting,

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:41.740
being insincere in his
acting, or actually being

00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:44.310
sincere about his manners?

00:28:44.310 --> 00:28:46.440
Everyone finds Frank
Churchill's manners pleasing.

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:48.630
But in the book, at
least, Emma can't quite

00:28:48.630 --> 00:28:51.462
figure out what he truly was.

00:28:51.462 --> 00:28:53.670
And we know that Elizabeth
Bennet's first impressions

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:57.180
of Wickham and Darcy,
formed during a ball,

00:28:57.180 --> 00:28:59.680
are badly mistaken.

00:28:59.680 --> 00:29:04.030
Like clichés and dead metaphors,
manners can dull the mind.

00:29:04.030 --> 00:29:07.810
Smooth style can charm, seduce,
and obscure, rather than

00:29:07.810 --> 00:29:09.810
reveal character.

00:29:09.810 --> 00:29:12.920
Austen's heroines need
training in hermeneutics,

00:29:12.920 --> 00:29:15.490
in interpretation,
in order to discern

00:29:15.490 --> 00:29:19.260
the moral substance of words and
gestures that they encounter.

00:29:19.260 --> 00:29:21.070
Austen's books are,
among other things,

00:29:21.070 --> 00:29:24.500
exercises in moral suspicion.

00:29:24.500 --> 00:29:28.260
By tracing the surface contours
of characters and situations,

00:29:28.260 --> 00:29:30.980
she trains her readers to
distinguish true actors

00:29:30.980 --> 00:29:33.890
from duplicitous ones.

00:29:33.890 --> 00:29:37.060
Second, Austen's measured
style models the kind of truth

00:29:37.060 --> 00:29:40.480
telling that's required to
live with moral integrity.

00:29:40.480 --> 00:29:42.670
Austen's characters
must learn, above all,

00:29:42.670 --> 00:29:44.890
to see and tell the
truth about themselves,

00:29:44.890 --> 00:29:50.270
to recognize, as James Bowman
said, when they are wrong.

00:29:50.270 --> 00:29:54.080
"I cannot express my own
abhorrence of myself,"

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:56.225
cries Marianne Dashwood.

00:29:56.225 --> 00:29:58.600
When Elizabeth Bennet recognizes
the truth about Wickham,

00:29:58.600 --> 00:29:59.890
she is devastated.

00:29:59.890 --> 00:30:02.640
"How humiliating
is this discovery!

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:06.620
Yet, how just a humiliation!"

00:30:06.620 --> 00:30:08.120
"How to understand it all?"

00:30:08.120 --> 00:30:12.350
Emma Woodhouse asks at the
moment of her illumination--

00:30:12.350 --> 00:30:14.030
how to understand
the deceptions she

00:30:14.030 --> 00:30:18.700
had been practicing on
herself and living under.

00:30:18.700 --> 00:30:21.190
We become truth-tellers,
at least in part,

00:30:21.190 --> 00:30:25.420
when we mimic the precision
of Austen's style.

00:30:25.420 --> 00:30:27.940
To tell the truth, we have
to renounce the mind-dulling

00:30:27.940 --> 00:30:33.600
metaphors and clichés that kill
thought and promote groupthink.

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:36.420
Truth-telling requires
the self-restraint

00:30:36.420 --> 00:30:40.710
to speak what we know, to fall
silent when we're ignorant,

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:44.200
and to check the emotions
that cloud our discernment.

00:30:44.200 --> 00:30:47.620
It demands that we do more than
express our passing sensations

00:30:47.620 --> 00:30:52.210
and whims, as Mrs. Elton was
doing in that passage I read.

00:30:52.210 --> 00:30:55.400
Truth-telling depends on
clarity and charity of vision,

00:30:55.400 --> 00:30:58.450
on courage and tact.

00:30:58.450 --> 00:31:02.083
I'm afraid I've made Austen
sound ponderous and priggish.

00:31:02.083 --> 00:31:04.250
Nothing, of course, could
be further from the truth.

00:31:04.250 --> 00:31:08.020
She is among the most
bemused and amusing writers

00:31:08.020 --> 00:31:12.340
in English literature, not in
spite of her moral firmness

00:31:12.340 --> 00:31:14.280
but because of it.

00:31:14.280 --> 00:31:17.070
As Lewis observed, "The hard
core of morality and even

00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:19.530
of religion seems to
me to be just what

00:31:19.530 --> 00:31:21.870
makes good comedy possible.

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:25.290
Were there no norm,
nothing can be ridiculous,

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:28.710
except for a brief moment
of unbalanced provincialism

00:31:28.710 --> 00:31:31.885
in which we may laugh at
the merely unfamiliar.

00:31:31.885 --> 00:31:34.260
Unless there is something
about which the author is never

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:39.705
ironical, there can be no
true irony in the work."

00:31:39.705 --> 00:31:41.080
So Austen's moral
clarity is what

00:31:41.080 --> 00:31:43.610
enables her to write
splendid scenes,

00:31:43.610 --> 00:31:45.610
like the one where Eleanor
and Marianne Dashwood

00:31:45.610 --> 00:31:49.030
first encounter Robert
Ferrars at a jewelry shop

00:31:49.030 --> 00:31:51.660
in Sense and Sensibility.

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:54.420
Robert, ignoring all
the other customers,

00:31:54.420 --> 00:32:00.500
is engaged in a grandiose quest
for the perfect toothpick case.

00:32:00.500 --> 00:32:03.020
This is from Sense
and Sensibility.

00:32:03.020 --> 00:32:05.450
"He was giving orders
for a toothpick case

00:32:05.450 --> 00:32:08.610
for himself, until
its size, shape,

00:32:08.610 --> 00:32:10.880
and ornaments were
determined, all of which,

00:32:10.880 --> 00:32:12.740
after examining and
debating for a quarter

00:32:12.740 --> 00:32:15.970
of an hour over every
toothpick case in the shop,

00:32:15.970 --> 00:32:19.113
were finally arranged by
his own inventive fancy.

00:32:19.113 --> 00:32:21.530
He had no leisure to bestow
any other attention on the two

00:32:21.530 --> 00:32:24.650
ladies than what was comprised
in three or four very

00:32:24.650 --> 00:32:29.060
broad stares, a kind of
notice which served to imprint

00:32:29.060 --> 00:32:30.860
on Eleanor the
remembrance of a person

00:32:30.860 --> 00:32:37.850
and face of strong, natural,
sterling insignificance,

00:32:37.850 --> 00:32:41.120
though adorned in the
first style of fashion.

00:32:41.120 --> 00:32:42.470
At last, the affair was decided.

00:32:42.470 --> 00:32:45.392
The ivory, the gold, the pearls
all received their appointment.

00:32:45.392 --> 00:32:47.600
And the gentleman, having
named the last day on which

00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:50.390
his existence could be
continued without the possession

00:32:50.390 --> 00:32:54.710
of this toothpick case, drew on
his glove with leisurely care,

00:32:54.710 --> 00:32:57.200
and bestowing another glance
on the Miss Dashwoods,

00:32:57.200 --> 00:33:00.050
but such a one as
seemed rather to demand

00:33:00.050 --> 00:33:02.690
than express
admiration, walked off

00:33:02.690 --> 00:33:09.680
with a happy air of real conceit
and affected indifference."

00:33:09.680 --> 00:33:12.737
That is a masterpiece
of comic writing,

00:33:12.737 --> 00:33:15.320
most particularly in the central
description of the impression

00:33:15.320 --> 00:33:18.170
that Robert leaves on Eleanor.

00:33:18.170 --> 00:33:20.180
When Austen follows a
string of adjectives

00:33:20.180 --> 00:33:27.740
like strong, natural, sterling,
with the noun "insignificance",

00:33:27.740 --> 00:33:31.780
you can hear Robert
Ferrar's deflating.

00:33:31.780 --> 00:33:34.510
He's a man of style
without substance,

00:33:34.510 --> 00:33:38.890
punctured by the repeated
pricks of Austen's moral comedy.

00:33:38.890 --> 00:33:41.410
Eleanor might well echo
Elizabeth Bennet's evaluation

00:33:41.410 --> 00:33:42.850
of Collins' letter.

00:33:42.850 --> 00:33:47.490
There is something pompous
in Robert's manners.

00:33:47.490 --> 00:33:50.130
In Austen, comedy,
morality, and style

00:33:50.130 --> 00:33:53.910
form a cord of three strands.

00:33:53.910 --> 00:33:56.370
Her stylistic miniaturism
focuses our attention

00:33:56.370 --> 00:34:00.570
on everyday life, where
her comic insight exposes

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:05.320
the absurd insubstantiality
of every mere facade.

00:34:05.320 --> 00:34:07.450
Charlotte Bronte
notwithstanding,

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:12.610
Austen's Chinese fidelity,
her miniature delicacy,

00:34:12.610 --> 00:34:15.550
her self-professed devotion
to fine brushwork on a sliver

00:34:15.550 --> 00:34:18.610
of ivory is not a defect.

00:34:18.610 --> 00:34:21.040
It's the vehicle
of her moral vision

00:34:21.040 --> 00:34:23.820
and the foundation
of her moral art.

00:34:23.820 --> 00:34:24.611
Thank you.

00:34:24.611 --> 00:34:27.016
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:35.210 --> 00:34:36.920
SPEAKER: Thank
you, Dr. Leithart.

00:34:36.920 --> 00:34:40.110
We do have some time for
Q&amp;A. If you have a question,

00:34:40.110 --> 00:34:44.639
please make your way to
one of the side aisles.

00:34:44.639 --> 00:34:46.445
AUDIENCE: So I think
what I'm going to say

00:34:46.445 --> 00:34:50.288
is instructive to young people.

00:34:50.288 --> 00:34:53.060
What does Emma represent?

00:34:53.060 --> 00:34:55.980
Well she's, on one
hand, a busybody.

00:34:55.980 --> 00:34:58.700
She's actively
talking with people.

00:34:58.700 --> 00:34:59.810
She finds out.

00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:03.590
She drives towards a goal,
sometimes delusional,

00:35:03.590 --> 00:35:05.810
but she wants to
accomplish something.

00:35:05.810 --> 00:35:07.430
What is the practical value?

00:35:07.430 --> 00:35:10.580
The practical value is,
for young people nowadays,

00:35:10.580 --> 00:35:13.580
is to be a busybody
but a different level.

00:35:13.580 --> 00:35:14.870
Read different things.

00:35:14.870 --> 00:35:15.870
Find out.

00:35:15.870 --> 00:35:16.970
Ask questions.

00:35:16.970 --> 00:35:18.440
Exchange ideas.

00:35:18.440 --> 00:35:21.660
Be a person who's active,
who's driving towards a goal.

00:35:21.660 --> 00:35:25.070
Through that process, you're
going to be standing out.

00:35:25.070 --> 00:35:26.405
You're going to accomplish more.

00:35:26.405 --> 00:35:27.530
You're going to be noticed.

00:35:27.530 --> 00:35:31.287
You're going to be
more successful.

00:35:31.287 --> 00:35:33.120
PETER LEITHART: I'll
take that as a comment.

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:33.948
Thank you.

00:35:33.948 --> 00:35:35.820
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:38.596 --> 00:35:39.096
Oh.

00:35:39.096 --> 00:35:40.020
AUDIENCE: Sure.

00:35:40.020 --> 00:35:40.520
Hi.

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:41.330
PETER LEITHART: I'm trying
to get out of the spotlight.

00:35:41.330 --> 00:35:42.538
That's why I'm moving around.

00:35:42.538 --> 00:35:43.080
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:35:43.080 --> 00:35:44.420
Thank you so much for your talk.

00:35:44.420 --> 00:35:46.580
It was amazing.

00:35:46.580 --> 00:35:50.900
I'm wondering, in your talk,
why is truth revealed in letters

00:35:50.900 --> 00:35:53.750
and particulars, for
example, the particular roles

00:35:53.750 --> 00:35:56.870
that people play in
society and status,

00:35:56.870 --> 00:36:00.620
if it should ideally
supersede these particulars

00:36:00.620 --> 00:36:02.787
and reach higher things,
more abstract things than--

00:36:02.787 --> 00:36:05.245
PETER LEITHART: Just the first
part again of your question.

00:36:05.245 --> 00:36:06.170
AUDIENCE: Sorry.

00:36:06.170 --> 00:36:09.560
Why is truth revealed
in Jane Austen's novels

00:36:09.560 --> 00:36:14.120
in letters and particulars,
if it should ideally

00:36:14.120 --> 00:36:17.813
supersede these constructs and
reach more abstract things,

00:36:17.813 --> 00:36:18.813
if you know what I mean?

00:36:18.813 --> 00:36:19.688
PETER LEITHART: Yeah.

00:36:19.688 --> 00:36:20.360
I think I know.

00:36:20.360 --> 00:36:25.600
But if I don't answer the
question, you can try it again.

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:29.830
Well, I think that Austen
definitely believes in

00:36:29.830 --> 00:36:33.240
and she uses abstract
moral categories.

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:35.150
And she uses them
a lot, the kind

00:36:35.150 --> 00:36:37.880
of abstract moral
categories that

00:36:37.880 --> 00:36:42.480
were the normal parlance
of moralists of her time.

00:36:42.480 --> 00:36:44.450
So it's not that
she avoids that,

00:36:44.450 --> 00:36:48.890
but she's never talking
about those merely

00:36:48.890 --> 00:36:50.030
in a theoretical fashion.

00:36:50.030 --> 00:36:56.090
But they're always exemplified
in particular situations

00:36:56.090 --> 00:36:59.510
with people who have particular
constraints on their behavior

00:36:59.510 --> 00:37:04.190
for various reasons
and are called

00:37:04.190 --> 00:37:06.870
to do certain things in
certain circumstances.

00:37:06.870 --> 00:37:10.310
So I'm not sure that
Austen's thinking

00:37:10.310 --> 00:37:14.750
is, you rise from the
particulars to the universals.

00:37:14.750 --> 00:37:16.340
That may be one part
of the movement,

00:37:16.340 --> 00:37:19.582
but I think it's
probably something more

00:37:19.582 --> 00:37:20.540
like a double movement.

00:37:20.540 --> 00:37:22.287
You have to rise
from particulars,

00:37:22.287 --> 00:37:23.870
and you do have to
work with some kind

00:37:23.870 --> 00:37:25.970
of general moral categories.

00:37:25.970 --> 00:37:28.220
But then, you also have to
go back to the particulars,

00:37:28.220 --> 00:37:31.070
because just living in these
general moral abstractions

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:32.960
is not living a moral life.

00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:35.353
So I think it's a
double movement.

00:37:35.353 --> 00:37:36.770
And the point I
was trying to make

00:37:36.770 --> 00:37:43.520
was the focus that she places
on the particulars as the place

00:37:43.520 --> 00:37:46.740
where those abstract
qualities are worked out.

00:37:46.740 --> 00:37:48.740
Is that getting at the
question you were asking?

00:37:48.740 --> 00:37:49.390
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:37:49.390 --> 00:37:49.970
PETER LEITHART: Yeah.

00:37:49.970 --> 00:37:50.470
Yeah.

00:37:50.470 --> 00:37:52.940
And were you asking
about the use of letters?

00:37:52.940 --> 00:37:54.641
You mentioned--

00:37:54.641 --> 00:37:56.060
AUDIENCE: OK, so yes.

00:37:56.060 --> 00:37:58.132
Letters like, for
example, the particulars

00:37:58.132 --> 00:37:59.840
that they go into in
some of the letters,

00:37:59.840 --> 00:38:02.720
like the mannerisms that are
conveyed through letters,

00:38:02.720 --> 00:38:05.270
giving people their
first impressions.

00:38:05.270 --> 00:38:07.250
Is this supposed to be truth?

00:38:07.250 --> 00:38:08.720
Is this supposed
to be how you find

00:38:08.720 --> 00:38:10.340
truth about certain people?

00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:13.070
Or is it supposed
to exceed, ideally

00:38:13.070 --> 00:38:15.405
for Austen, those letters?

00:38:15.405 --> 00:38:16.280
PETER LEITHART: Yeah.

00:38:16.280 --> 00:38:18.320
Again, yeah, my point.

00:38:18.320 --> 00:38:20.000
I think it's a kind
of double movement.

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:23.390
I do think it's
interesting how much

00:38:23.390 --> 00:38:26.900
of the action of Austen's
novels turns on the reception

00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:29.030
and delivery of letters.

00:38:29.030 --> 00:38:34.573
And, of course, this was a
novelistic style of the time

00:38:34.573 --> 00:38:36.740
that she experimented with
in some of her early work

00:38:36.740 --> 00:38:38.480
with epistolary novels.

00:38:38.480 --> 00:38:41.090
James Bowman made passing
reference to this last evening.

00:38:41.090 --> 00:38:45.920
But even when she's not
writing an epistolary novel,

00:38:45.920 --> 00:38:50.210
Elizabeth Bennet's recognition
of Darcy's character

00:38:50.210 --> 00:38:53.570
partly comes from
visiting his home

00:38:53.570 --> 00:38:55.793
and imagining herself as
the lady of that house.

00:38:55.793 --> 00:38:57.710
But it also comes from
a letter that he gives.

00:38:57.710 --> 00:39:00.110
And I think there's
something about this message

00:39:00.110 --> 00:39:01.910
from a distance.

00:39:01.910 --> 00:39:04.100
And it leaves the
character alone

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:07.100
with this new information,
rather than always--

00:39:07.100 --> 00:39:10.220
and sometimes it's in an
interpersonal conflict,

00:39:10.220 --> 00:39:17.960
interpersonal exchange, like
Mr. Knightley's chiding of Emma.

00:39:17.960 --> 00:39:19.940
But that takes place in person.

00:39:19.940 --> 00:39:22.570
But a lot of the new
information comes from outside,

00:39:22.570 --> 00:39:24.890
and the character's
left alone to try

00:39:24.890 --> 00:39:27.590
to assess this new
information, try to adjust,

00:39:27.590 --> 00:39:30.440
recognize the wrongness of
what they had been thinking,

00:39:30.440 --> 00:39:31.310
in isolation.

00:39:34.160 --> 00:39:36.668
AUDIENCE: Yeah, so this may
be less of a writing style

00:39:36.668 --> 00:39:38.710
question and more of a
life application question.

00:39:38.710 --> 00:39:41.545
You mentioned 15 minutes
into the lecture,

00:39:41.545 --> 00:39:43.420
most of the characters
have this hardy relish

00:39:43.420 --> 00:39:45.560
for modest pleasures.

00:39:45.560 --> 00:39:46.450
So I'm a psych major.

00:39:46.450 --> 00:39:47.830
So one of the things that
we talk about all the time

00:39:47.830 --> 00:39:50.110
is how our generation,
the previous generation,

00:39:50.110 --> 00:39:52.000
some of their biggest
qualms with life

00:39:52.000 --> 00:39:54.550
is a general lack of meaning
or a general lack of pleasure

00:39:54.550 --> 00:39:55.390
in things.

00:39:55.390 --> 00:39:58.070
It seems too finite,
in many ways.

00:39:58.070 --> 00:40:01.317
So would you chalk
that down to there's

00:40:01.317 --> 00:40:03.400
no real appreciation for
modest pleasures anymore,

00:40:03.400 --> 00:40:05.525
or just a general lack of
modesty in society today?

00:40:08.020 --> 00:40:10.520
PETER LEITHART: Yeah, I think
that's a really good question.

00:40:10.520 --> 00:40:12.478
It's hard to generalize
about what's happening.

00:40:12.478 --> 00:40:16.750
I think, a couple of
things I would say.

00:40:16.750 --> 00:40:21.712
I'm going beyond Austen
here, expressing my own views

00:40:21.712 --> 00:40:23.170
and saying it the
way I would have.

00:40:23.170 --> 00:40:25.960
I think there's
probably something

00:40:25.960 --> 00:40:31.450
like a metaphysical deficit
in that experience, that

00:40:31.450 --> 00:40:38.770
is a failure to appreciate
what reality is.

00:40:38.770 --> 00:40:42.040
And I think there's a
theological and religious

00:40:42.040 --> 00:40:43.690
dimension to that.

00:40:43.690 --> 00:40:49.540
If you believe that reality
is, from top to bottom,

00:40:49.540 --> 00:40:56.500
a gift of an infinite god,
then the smallest delight

00:40:56.500 --> 00:41:00.110
is worthy of your
attention and pleasure.

00:41:00.110 --> 00:41:02.370
And if you don't, if you
think that this is just

00:41:02.370 --> 00:41:06.820
a product of chance and
long periods of time,

00:41:06.820 --> 00:41:10.510
then you have a
different metaphysics,

00:41:10.510 --> 00:41:11.620
a different conception.

00:41:11.620 --> 00:41:13.900
And I also think of a
number of theologians

00:41:13.900 --> 00:41:16.270
of late have been
calling attention

00:41:16.270 --> 00:41:19.480
to the medieval notion of sloth.

00:41:19.480 --> 00:41:20.525
The term is acedia.

00:41:24.430 --> 00:41:29.710
It would be the term that
Dante uses in the Inferno.

00:41:29.710 --> 00:41:34.870
Acedia's not just laziness, but
acedia is just a complete lack

00:41:34.870 --> 00:41:38.440
of delight in the world.

00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:40.180
It's kind of the
"whatever mentality.

00:41:40.180 --> 00:41:41.352
I've done that.

00:41:41.352 --> 00:41:43.060
There's nothing in
the world that's going

00:41:43.060 --> 00:41:45.140
to interest or delight me.

00:41:45.140 --> 00:41:49.450
And I think there's a jadedness
to, maybe, particularly younger

00:41:49.450 --> 00:41:50.120
generations.

00:41:50.120 --> 00:41:52.537
I don't know if that's the
case, but it may have something

00:41:52.537 --> 00:41:56.260
to do with the ways the
technologies have taken over,

00:41:56.260 --> 00:41:59.630
the lack of contact with
an unmediated world.

00:41:59.630 --> 00:42:01.690
I think there's
something in that.

00:42:01.690 --> 00:42:05.221
But there's this general
mood of acedia that, I think,

00:42:05.221 --> 00:42:06.620
has a number of different roots.

00:42:06.620 --> 00:42:08.680
But that's how I
would describe it.

00:42:12.830 --> 00:42:14.580
AUDIENCE: I was
interested by your point--

00:42:14.580 --> 00:42:16.340
and I definitely saw
the relevance of it--

00:42:16.340 --> 00:42:19.340
how Austen handles
morality as applied

00:42:19.340 --> 00:42:21.290
to what is expected
of someone as a member

00:42:21.290 --> 00:42:24.890
of a certain social class
or a certain standing.

00:42:24.890 --> 00:42:27.020
So I'm curious on your
thoughts on the end

00:42:27.020 --> 00:42:28.040
of Pride and Prejudice.

00:42:28.040 --> 00:42:30.650
Even though things with
Lydia are patched up,

00:42:30.650 --> 00:42:32.510
clearly something
wasn't right there.

00:42:32.510 --> 00:42:36.387
So is Darcy essentially
going against that norm?

00:42:36.387 --> 00:42:37.970
Or do we think
Austen's saying there's

00:42:37.970 --> 00:42:41.750
something stronger and different
and even more valuable to what

00:42:41.750 --> 00:42:45.740
he did, instead of just avoiding
her family because of that?

00:42:45.740 --> 00:42:46.668
PETER LEITHART: Yeah.

00:42:46.668 --> 00:42:48.710
I agree with what James
Bowman said last evening,

00:42:48.710 --> 00:42:53.150
that she took the class
system for granted.

00:42:53.150 --> 00:42:54.920
I don't think she's
a critic of it.

00:42:54.920 --> 00:43:00.380
But I think we can misunderstand
what the class system is about

00:43:00.380 --> 00:43:07.910
and particularly
misunderstand the obligations

00:43:07.910 --> 00:43:15.935
that upper classes had in a
traditional English society.

00:43:18.440 --> 00:43:21.050
Being a master of a
great house was not just

00:43:21.050 --> 00:43:23.570
a privilege, a
privilege of wealth

00:43:23.570 --> 00:43:28.430
and the possibility of command,
but it also imposed obligations

00:43:28.430 --> 00:43:34.380
to care for those who
were under your authority

00:43:34.380 --> 00:43:37.040
or dependent on you in some way.

00:43:37.040 --> 00:43:39.170
Mr. Knightley, I think,
is a great illustration

00:43:39.170 --> 00:43:43.760
of that kind of
nobility as a status,

00:43:43.760 --> 00:43:45.770
but also as a character trait.

00:43:45.770 --> 00:43:48.590
And it involves charity.

00:43:48.590 --> 00:43:50.990
And it involves
things like what Darcy

00:43:50.990 --> 00:43:55.010
does to resolve the scandal
of the Bennet family.

00:43:55.010 --> 00:43:58.490
I think that's an act
of excessive charity,

00:43:58.490 --> 00:44:00.920
obviously coming out of
his affection for Elizabeth

00:44:00.920 --> 00:44:02.120
in large part.

00:44:02.120 --> 00:44:04.003
But it's also an act
of charity that's

00:44:04.003 --> 00:44:06.170
consistent with the obligations
that would be placed

00:44:06.170 --> 00:44:08.270
on somebody in his status.

00:44:08.270 --> 00:44:15.260
So yeah, I think we tend to
misunderstand what status

00:44:15.260 --> 00:44:18.000
is about in Austen's time.

00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:21.480
It had both obligations and
privileges attached to it.

00:44:25.180 --> 00:44:28.500
AUDIENCE: So you mentioned
that people in Austen's day

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:34.350
had to perceive someone's
character through good manners

00:44:34.350 --> 00:44:36.120
and dialogue.

00:44:36.120 --> 00:44:38.940
And then you also
talked about how

00:44:38.940 --> 00:44:41.100
Austen describes a lot of
the scenes and characters

00:44:41.100 --> 00:44:43.838
through dialogue.

00:44:43.838 --> 00:44:46.380
Do you think that she's trying
to get her readers to practice

00:44:46.380 --> 00:44:50.570
that same perception of
characters and of the situation

00:44:50.570 --> 00:44:53.850
from the dialogue that her
heroines have to practice?

00:44:53.850 --> 00:44:55.020
PETER LEITHART: Yes, I do.

00:44:55.020 --> 00:45:02.960
I think that this is a kind of
ancient notion of the purposes

00:45:02.960 --> 00:45:05.250
of literature, which
is never simply

00:45:05.250 --> 00:45:08.230
to delight, but to
delight and instruct.

00:45:08.230 --> 00:45:10.560
So I think that the
design is not just

00:45:10.560 --> 00:45:14.910
to tell a good story, to
elicit the oohs and the ahs

00:45:14.910 --> 00:45:17.130
that I heard in the
audience during the closing

00:45:17.130 --> 00:45:18.240
scenes of Emma.

00:45:18.240 --> 00:45:19.732
That's part of it.

00:45:19.732 --> 00:45:21.690
She wants to write stories
that are interesting

00:45:21.690 --> 00:45:24.060
and that have this kind
of emotional charge,

00:45:24.060 --> 00:45:25.530
but she intends to instruct.

00:45:25.530 --> 00:45:32.190
And she's trying to provide
her readers with tools, maybe

00:45:32.190 --> 00:45:38.490
models, in order to discern
between, as I described it,

00:45:38.490 --> 00:45:41.730
the true actors and
duplicitous ones.

00:45:41.730 --> 00:45:47.840
Stanley Fish, kind of a
radical critic, wrote, I think,

00:45:47.840 --> 00:45:48.480
his first book.

00:45:48.480 --> 00:45:50.655
He was a Milton
scholar early on.

00:45:50.655 --> 00:45:53.010
And one of his first books,
I think his first book,

00:45:53.010 --> 00:45:54.410
was called Surprised by Sin.

00:45:54.410 --> 00:45:56.280
It's about Paradise Lost.

00:45:56.280 --> 00:46:02.280
And his argument was that the
attraction of Satan in Paradise

00:46:02.280 --> 00:46:04.590
Lost is not an error
on Milton's part,

00:46:04.590 --> 00:46:09.375
but it's part of the
design of the poem.

00:46:09.375 --> 00:46:13.470
You have romantic readers,
like William Blake,

00:46:13.470 --> 00:46:16.640
who think that Satan is
the hero of the poem.

00:46:16.640 --> 00:46:19.770
Fish says that that's not
at all Milton's intention.

00:46:19.770 --> 00:46:22.680
And Milton didn't lose
control of his character.

00:46:22.680 --> 00:46:24.540
He wants to make
Satan attractive,

00:46:24.540 --> 00:46:25.980
because he wants
to put the reader

00:46:25.980 --> 00:46:28.650
through the same
experience of temptation

00:46:28.650 --> 00:46:31.210
that the characters
are going through.

00:46:31.210 --> 00:46:34.260
And I think that kind
of moral rhetoric

00:46:34.260 --> 00:46:35.920
is part of Austen's design, too.

00:46:35.920 --> 00:46:38.340
She's trying to put her
readers through the same kind

00:46:38.340 --> 00:46:41.910
of process of
discernment, illumination

00:46:41.910 --> 00:46:44.250
of their own feelings.

00:46:44.250 --> 00:46:47.180
So I think there's definitely
that moral character to it.

00:46:51.170 --> 00:46:54.190
AUDIENCE: So you mentioned
the Thirty-nine Articles

00:46:54.190 --> 00:46:56.500
and the Church of England.

00:46:56.500 --> 00:47:01.090
But reading Austen's novels and
watching the movies especially,

00:47:01.090 --> 00:47:05.245
I don't see much religion
explicitly mentioned.

00:47:05.245 --> 00:47:06.370
Is there a reason for that?

00:47:06.370 --> 00:47:10.090
And would you say
that those particulars

00:47:10.090 --> 00:47:13.450
in Jane Austen's life,
those aspects of her life,

00:47:13.450 --> 00:47:17.890
that they affected her
novels in subtle ways?

00:47:17.890 --> 00:47:19.890
And how did they do that?

00:47:19.890 --> 00:47:20.860
PETER LEITHART: Yeah.

00:47:20.860 --> 00:47:21.920
A couple of things.

00:47:21.920 --> 00:47:26.650
First of all, I think there's
more religious interest

00:47:26.650 --> 00:47:30.950
overtly in Austen's novels
than we sometimes recognize.

00:47:30.950 --> 00:47:33.100
There are a lot of clergymen.

00:47:33.100 --> 00:47:36.730
Some of the clergymen
are parodied--

00:47:36.730 --> 00:47:39.160
Collins and Elton,
particularly--

00:47:39.160 --> 00:47:40.720
but not all of them.

00:47:40.720 --> 00:47:43.750
I mean, there's a long
Christian tradition

00:47:43.750 --> 00:47:45.880
of poking fun at the clergy.

00:47:45.880 --> 00:47:49.660
I mean, that's not a new
thing with Jane Austen.

00:47:49.660 --> 00:47:54.280
But you also have characters
like Edmund Bertram

00:47:54.280 --> 00:47:56.260
in Mansfield Park,
which is, I think,

00:47:56.260 --> 00:47:58.990
Mansfield Park is the
most overtly theological

00:47:58.990 --> 00:48:01.630
and religious of her novels.

00:48:01.630 --> 00:48:06.550
And there are conversations
between Mary Crawford

00:48:06.550 --> 00:48:13.300
and Edmund Bertram about
the vocation of a minister.

00:48:13.300 --> 00:48:15.790
Mary Crawford is a
kind of temptress

00:48:15.790 --> 00:48:20.230
trying to divert Edmund from
his vocation, which is to enter

00:48:20.230 --> 00:48:21.820
the ministry and be a priest.

00:48:21.820 --> 00:48:23.740
And he sees his role
as being one of--

00:48:27.500 --> 00:48:30.700
it's not a high
position socially,

00:48:30.700 --> 00:48:37.750
but he sees it as crucial
to developing communities

00:48:37.750 --> 00:48:40.360
and developing
moral human beings.

00:48:40.360 --> 00:48:42.220
So he sees it as a
very high calling,

00:48:42.220 --> 00:48:44.440
and Mary Crawford is
trying to belittle it.

00:48:44.440 --> 00:48:46.623
So you have some overt
discussions like that.

00:48:46.623 --> 00:48:48.040
And there have
been some critics--

00:48:48.040 --> 00:48:49.665
and I think there's
something to this--

00:48:49.665 --> 00:48:52.240
there have been some
critics who have suggested--

00:48:52.240 --> 00:48:56.170
I don't remember who it was--
but described Austen's romances

00:48:56.170 --> 00:48:57.475
as being Anglican romances.

00:49:03.538 --> 00:49:05.080
There are various
considerations that

00:49:05.080 --> 00:49:09.280
go into the resolution
of a romance in Austen.

00:49:09.280 --> 00:49:11.600
Obviously, there's attraction.

00:49:11.600 --> 00:49:16.450
There's compatibility
of taste and temper.

00:49:16.450 --> 00:49:21.015
Money is an important--
and that's not cynical.

00:49:21.015 --> 00:49:23.140
She knows that money is an
important consideration.

00:49:23.140 --> 00:49:24.880
But there's also
something happens

00:49:24.880 --> 00:49:28.060
that's just inexplicable.

00:49:28.060 --> 00:49:30.130
There's this kind of--

00:49:30.130 --> 00:49:32.930
I don't know-- you
call it magic or grace,

00:49:32.930 --> 00:49:35.770
though you shouldn't use
that terminology, not often.

00:49:35.770 --> 00:49:37.420
But there's
something else that's

00:49:37.420 --> 00:49:40.242
active in those romances that
brings out the happy ending.

00:49:40.242 --> 00:49:41.700
So I think there's
something to it.

00:49:44.560 --> 00:49:48.790
It is background in
many of her novels.

00:49:48.790 --> 00:49:51.293
It's not foregrounded,
but I think it's there.

00:49:51.293 --> 00:49:52.960
And just personally,
I mean, she grew up

00:49:52.960 --> 00:49:57.490
in a home of a Church
of England priest.

00:49:57.490 --> 00:50:02.308
She had brothers
who were priests

00:50:02.308 --> 00:50:03.350
in the Church of England.

00:50:03.350 --> 00:50:04.392
She was surrounded by it.

00:50:06.655 --> 00:50:08.530
Her entire life was in
the Church of England.

00:50:08.530 --> 00:50:11.710
So it doesn't come out overtly.

00:50:11.710 --> 00:50:15.280
That may also be the
Anglicanism of her romances,

00:50:15.280 --> 00:50:17.350
because Anglicans tend
to be a little bit

00:50:17.350 --> 00:50:18.910
reticent about their faith.

00:50:21.810 --> 00:50:24.060
They don't tend to be as
effusive as evangelicals are.

00:50:27.655 --> 00:50:30.170
AUDIENCE: Dr. Leithart, in
answer to another student's

00:50:30.170 --> 00:50:33.380
question, you mentioned
that Jane Austen frequently

00:50:33.380 --> 00:50:35.780
utilizes the message
from a distance

00:50:35.780 --> 00:50:39.200
in order to make her characters
encounter a moral challenge.

00:50:39.200 --> 00:50:41.630
However, in your talk,
you also mentioned

00:50:41.630 --> 00:50:43.700
that the greatest
moral challenges occur

00:50:43.700 --> 00:50:45.540
in the midst of daily life.

00:50:45.540 --> 00:50:47.805
So I'm curious
what you believe is

00:50:47.805 --> 00:50:51.020
the significance in
connection to those two,

00:50:51.020 --> 00:50:53.390
how she makes the
characters make

00:50:53.390 --> 00:50:55.250
the decision within
a social setting,

00:50:55.250 --> 00:50:57.975
but also encounter those
challenges in solitude?

00:50:57.975 --> 00:50:58.850
PETER LEITHART: Yeah.

00:50:58.850 --> 00:51:00.500
Yeah, well, I wouldn't put
those two in opposition

00:51:00.500 --> 00:51:01.140
to each other.

00:51:01.140 --> 00:51:06.140
I think that the letter that
Elizabeth Bennet receives,

00:51:06.140 --> 00:51:09.020
for example, is
about the things that

00:51:09.020 --> 00:51:12.530
have happened in those
different social settings,

00:51:12.530 --> 00:51:15.650
the misjudgments
that she's made,

00:51:15.650 --> 00:51:17.570
the wrong impressions
that she's formed

00:51:17.570 --> 00:51:19.760
about different characters.

00:51:19.760 --> 00:51:22.970
And there's that moment
of her being isolated,

00:51:22.970 --> 00:51:26.390
but it's integrated into
that larger social setting.

00:51:26.390 --> 00:51:29.200
So I wouldn't put those
two in opposition.

00:51:29.200 --> 00:51:32.000
Yeah, they're different moments
within the everyday life

00:51:32.000 --> 00:51:35.920
that they're experiencing.

00:51:35.920 --> 00:51:39.670
Did you want a follow up?

00:51:39.670 --> 00:51:42.910
AUDIENCE: So is it
necessary for each character

00:51:42.910 --> 00:51:46.540
to have that solitude
after, in order

00:51:46.540 --> 00:51:48.670
to process the social setting?

00:51:48.670 --> 00:51:50.470
Or not necessarily?

00:51:50.470 --> 00:51:52.990
PETER LEITHART: Yeah, I'd have
to think more about specifics

00:51:52.990 --> 00:51:54.573
to know if that
happens in every case.

00:51:54.573 --> 00:52:00.130
I think there are different
degrees of it, I guess.

00:52:00.130 --> 00:52:03.930
I'd have to think
through examples.

00:52:03.930 --> 00:52:04.430
Thanks.

00:52:07.150 --> 00:52:07.920
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:52:07.920 --> 00:52:11.785
Just a particular Pride
and Prejudice question.

00:52:11.785 --> 00:52:15.040
Toward the end of the
novel, as Elizabeth

00:52:15.040 --> 00:52:17.560
is kind of waking up to
the fact that Darcy might

00:52:17.560 --> 00:52:20.950
be worth her while, she
reflects pretty critically

00:52:20.950 --> 00:52:22.990
on her parents.

00:52:22.990 --> 00:52:26.630
And I think her father
comes out better for it,

00:52:26.630 --> 00:52:30.750
but her mother comes
out worse for the wear.

00:52:30.750 --> 00:52:32.290
And in one sense,
what you can see

00:52:32.290 --> 00:52:36.700
is she's so acute to the
way in which her mother sins

00:52:36.700 --> 00:52:38.740
against social norms.

00:52:38.740 --> 00:52:42.220
And so she's understanding
how her mother in her own way

00:52:42.220 --> 00:52:43.600
is a kind of bore.

00:52:43.600 --> 00:52:48.610
In another, it seems
like such a betrayal

00:52:48.610 --> 00:52:51.040
of another kind of
local thing that we

00:52:51.040 --> 00:52:53.560
should care about, which is
the relationship that one has

00:52:53.560 --> 00:52:58.020
with their mother and the
particularly good way in which

00:52:58.020 --> 00:53:00.970
a mother has reared
her daughter,

00:53:00.970 --> 00:53:03.965
I'm just curious as to
what you think about it.

00:53:03.965 --> 00:53:05.840
PETER LEITHART: Yeah,
that's a good question.

00:53:05.840 --> 00:53:09.340
And I'd have to think more
about how to sort through that.

00:53:09.340 --> 00:53:12.020
A couple of things occur to me.

00:53:12.020 --> 00:53:13.810
One is that I think
the part of what's

00:53:13.810 --> 00:53:15.550
going on in those
latter scenes is

00:53:15.550 --> 00:53:20.410
that Elizabeth is beginning to
see her family through Darcy's

00:53:20.410 --> 00:53:21.850
eyes.

00:53:21.850 --> 00:53:26.530
And she recognizes the
flaws in her family in a way

00:53:26.530 --> 00:53:27.610
that she hadn't before.

00:53:27.610 --> 00:53:32.440
And it makes sense to her why
Darcy would have warned Bingley

00:53:32.440 --> 00:53:34.610
off from her family.

00:53:34.610 --> 00:53:39.430
So I think that it's part of
the process of her falling

00:53:39.430 --> 00:53:42.550
in love with Darcy,
their becoming one,

00:53:42.550 --> 00:53:46.420
taking on a united mind.

00:53:46.420 --> 00:53:48.760
And it's also part
of her recognition

00:53:48.760 --> 00:53:50.770
of her own circumstances.

00:53:50.770 --> 00:53:53.080
I don't think there's anything--

00:53:53.080 --> 00:53:55.145
I'd have to look at the
particular passages.

00:53:55.145 --> 00:53:57.020
I don't think there's
anything disrespectful.

00:53:57.020 --> 00:53:58.000
I think it's truthful.

00:53:58.000 --> 00:54:02.200
And I'm not sure I'd agree that
her mother comes off worse.

00:54:02.200 --> 00:54:06.430
I think her dad
is a lot funnier.

00:54:06.430 --> 00:54:09.940
Well, her mother's pretty
funny, but her dad's

00:54:09.940 --> 00:54:13.090
a lot more calm and laid back.

00:54:13.090 --> 00:54:17.920
But the mess in the family
is largely his negligence,

00:54:17.920 --> 00:54:18.940
you could say.

00:54:18.940 --> 00:54:23.380
I had a student years ago
who did a senior thesis

00:54:23.380 --> 00:54:27.130
on the absent fathers
in Austen's novels,

00:54:27.130 --> 00:54:29.800
which is a recurring theme.

00:54:29.800 --> 00:54:35.410
And I think, yeah, Mr.
Bennet is willing to retire

00:54:35.410 --> 00:54:37.360
into his library
with a book, when he

00:54:37.360 --> 00:54:38.812
should be doing something else.

00:54:38.812 --> 00:54:41.020
He's willing for somebody
else to clean up the messes

00:54:41.020 --> 00:54:42.940
that his family makes.

00:54:42.940 --> 00:54:45.190
And you had that passage
that James Bowman talked

00:54:45.190 --> 00:54:47.920
about last night
where Mr. Bennet has

00:54:47.920 --> 00:54:52.480
this moment of a
sort of penitance,

00:54:52.480 --> 00:54:55.960
but he realizes
that it will pass

00:54:55.960 --> 00:54:59.110
and that he'll go back and read
a book in his library again.

00:54:59.110 --> 00:55:04.227
So I don't see it
as a disrespect.

00:55:04.227 --> 00:55:05.810
I'd have to look at
it more carefully,

00:55:05.810 --> 00:55:07.960
but I wouldn't see
it as disrespect.

00:55:07.960 --> 00:55:12.050
But it is an insight into the
faults of her family, which

00:55:12.050 --> 00:55:16.690
I don't think that's a matter
of disrespect or dishonor

00:55:16.690 --> 00:55:18.050
to her parents.

00:55:18.050 --> 00:55:21.412
SPEAKER: We have time
for one more question.

00:55:21.412 --> 00:55:22.870
AUDIENCE: All right,
last question.

00:55:22.870 --> 00:55:23.570
Promise.

00:55:23.570 --> 00:55:25.030
So as far as comedy
today, it's--

00:55:25.030 --> 00:55:25.850
PETER LEITHART: You
asked a question before?

00:55:25.850 --> 00:55:26.030
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:55:26.030 --> 00:55:26.940
PETER LEITHART: You again?

00:55:26.940 --> 00:55:27.565
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:55:27.565 --> 00:55:28.341
[LAUGHTER]

00:55:28.341 --> 00:55:28.897
I'm Mark.

00:55:28.897 --> 00:55:29.980
PETER LEITHART: Hey, Mark.

00:55:29.980 --> 00:55:31.105
AUDIENCE: Nice to meet you.

00:55:31.105 --> 00:55:32.680
So yeah, you as well.

00:55:33.190 --> 00:55:35.740
AUDIENCE: Yeah, well, hey,
best for last, I guess.

00:55:35.740 --> 00:55:37.748
Anyway, so as far as
comedy today, a lot of it's

00:55:37.748 --> 00:55:39.040
based on instant gratification.

00:55:39.040 --> 00:55:41.947
If you look at movie numbers
for the last seven years or so,

00:55:41.947 --> 00:55:43.780
there hasn't really
been a successful comedy

00:55:43.780 --> 00:55:46.012
movie, especially one
that's well rated.

00:55:46.012 --> 00:55:48.220
As well as what we're talking
about with Jane Austen,

00:55:48.220 --> 00:55:50.770
how she has a lot
of subtle, implied

00:55:50.770 --> 00:55:53.380
practical comedy in
all of her writing,

00:55:53.380 --> 00:55:55.330
do you think that
could be perhaps

00:55:55.330 --> 00:55:57.680
why, at least people today
have a difficult time--

00:55:57.680 --> 00:56:00.070
or younger people today have
a difficult time finding

00:56:00.070 --> 00:56:00.570
that humor?

00:56:00.570 --> 00:56:03.070
Because for me, personally, my
mom had to give me a heads-up

00:56:03.070 --> 00:56:04.960
beforehand that Jane
Austen was funny,

00:56:04.960 --> 00:56:06.910
before I took the
lecture series.

00:56:06.910 --> 00:56:09.285
And that's just someone who's
watched Pride and Prejudice

00:56:09.285 --> 00:56:10.695
three times with her before.

00:56:10.695 --> 00:56:12.820
PETER LEITHART: Well, I'm
glad your mother gave you

00:56:12.820 --> 00:56:13.810
the heads-up, Mark.

00:56:13.810 --> 00:56:15.120
That's good.

00:56:15.120 --> 00:56:15.760
Yeah.

00:56:15.760 --> 00:56:19.700
If you don't realize that,
then you're in trouble.

00:56:19.700 --> 00:56:20.410
Yeah.

00:56:20.410 --> 00:56:24.340
Well, I don't know if Whit
Stillman is in the room,

00:56:24.340 --> 00:56:25.810
but I think that,
if you're looking

00:56:25.810 --> 00:56:30.160
for Austen-esque humor
in films, his films are

00:56:30.160 --> 00:56:31.570
among the best recent films.

00:56:31.570 --> 00:56:34.660
I mean, the one straight
Austen adaptation he did,

00:56:34.660 --> 00:56:38.790
Love &amp; Friendship, which is
an adaptation of Lady Susan,

00:56:38.790 --> 00:56:42.310
is hilarious not just because
the Lady Susan character

00:56:42.310 --> 00:56:46.090
is so deliciously
evil, but there

00:56:46.090 --> 00:56:50.650
are some really, really amusing,
bombastic other characters

00:56:50.650 --> 00:56:52.800
in the film.

00:56:52.800 --> 00:56:56.110
Well, Austen's style of humor,
the passage I read at the end

00:56:56.110 --> 00:56:59.920
from Sense and Sensibility, a
lot of that depends on the way

00:56:59.920 --> 00:57:02.990
that Austen describes
what's happening.

00:57:02.990 --> 00:57:06.820
So the situation is
already ridiculous,

00:57:06.820 --> 00:57:10.920
because you have this
very wealthy man who's

00:57:10.920 --> 00:57:13.780
spending an enormous
amount of time and energy

00:57:13.780 --> 00:57:19.780
to get the exact design of
this very trivial object

00:57:19.780 --> 00:57:22.330
that he's interested
in, inconveniencing

00:57:22.330 --> 00:57:25.090
everyone else around him
in order to get this.

00:57:25.090 --> 00:57:29.110
So you have that kind of
comic folly that's there,

00:57:29.110 --> 00:57:32.290
but a lot of it is in the
way that she describes it

00:57:32.290 --> 00:57:33.970
and describes Robert.

00:57:33.970 --> 00:57:37.180
So that's very hard to
translate into film, I think.

00:57:37.180 --> 00:57:40.100
And so, yeah.

00:57:42.860 --> 00:57:45.340
maybe I'll go back
to another thing

00:57:45.340 --> 00:57:49.150
that I mentioned in the paper.

00:57:49.150 --> 00:57:52.780
I think maybe Lewis's discussion
of the religious and moral

00:57:52.780 --> 00:57:55.070
ground for comedy
would be as good

00:57:55.070 --> 00:57:57.680
an explanation as anything.

00:57:57.680 --> 00:58:02.290
And his point was that
you need something

00:58:02.290 --> 00:58:06.340
about which you are
never ironical in order

00:58:06.340 --> 00:58:08.260
to have irony work.

00:58:08.260 --> 00:58:10.720
And we live in a world where
irony just goes all the way

00:58:10.720 --> 00:58:12.160
down to the ground.

00:58:12.160 --> 00:58:16.240
There is nothing that
doesn't dissolve by irony.

00:58:16.240 --> 00:58:17.510
Austen is not like that.

00:58:17.510 --> 00:58:19.860
And so the best
humorists are going

00:58:19.860 --> 00:58:24.220
to be the ones that
have some kind of firm

00:58:24.220 --> 00:58:26.710
convictions about the world
so they can, as Lewis says,

00:58:26.710 --> 00:58:29.900
so they can recognize the
ridiculous when it happens.

00:58:29.900 --> 00:58:36.310
And I think that, maybe, that
moral or religious groundwork

00:58:36.310 --> 00:58:40.140
is what's lacking in a lot
of contemporary comedy,

00:58:40.140 --> 00:58:42.640
because you need to have
some standard of comparison

00:58:42.640 --> 00:58:47.320
in order to show that
something is absurd.

00:58:47.320 --> 00:58:47.920
Thanks.

00:58:47.920 --> 00:58:50.670
[APPLAUSE]

